      contentMarkdown:
        "## Two patterns, traced step by step\n\nThis walkthrough traces the exact code needed for two interview patterns that trip up candidates: the max-heap priority scheduler and the sliding window maximum with a monotonic deque. Both are O(n log n) or O(n) — the heap and the monotonic structure are what make them fast.\n\n## Pattern 1: Max-heap task scheduler\n\n**Problem:** given priorities=[3,1,2] and names=['indexer','logger','monitor'], process tasks highest-priority first.\n\n**Step 1: build the max-heap by negating priorities.**\n\n\`\`\`python\nimport heapq\n\ndef process_by_priority(priorities, names):\n    heap = [(-p, name) for p, name in zip(priorities, names)]\n    heapq.heapify(heap)   # O(n) — rearranges in-place\n    result = []\n    while heap:\n        _, name = heapq.heappop(heap)  # O(log n) — returns smallest neg priority\n        result.append(name)\n    return result\n\`\`\`\n\n**Step 2: trace heapify and pops.**\n\nAfter `heapify([(-3,'indexer'),(-1,'logger'),(-2,'monitor')])`:\nInternal heap order (min-heap of negated values, so -3 is the root): `[(-3,'indexer'), (-1,'logger'), (-2,'monitor')]`\n\n- Pop 1: extract `(-3, 'indexer')` → result=['indexer']. Heap: `[(-2,'monitor'), (-1,'logger')]`\n- Pop 2: extract `(-2, 'monitor')` → result=['indexer','monitor']. Heap: `[(-1,'logger')]`\n- Pop 3: extract `(-1, 'logger')` → result=['indexer','monitor','logger']\n\n**Result: ['indexer', 'monitor', 'logger']** — highest real priority first.\n\n**Why this generalizes:** in a live job queue where new tasks arrive continuously, you would `heappush(heap, (-priority, name))` for each arrival. The heap always gives you the highest-priority waiting task in O(log n) — you cannot do this with a sorted list because insertions into a sorted list are O(n).\n\n## Pattern 2: Sliding window maximum with a monotonic deque\n\n**Problem:** `rolling-window-max-latency` — given an array of latency measurements and a window size k, return the maximum in each window of size k.\n\n**The naive approach (wrong for this problem's constraints):** scan the entire window on each step — O(nk). For n=10^5 and k=10^3, that is 10^8 operations.\n\n**The monotonic deque approach: O(n).**\n\nMaintain a deque of *indices* in the current window, in *decreasing value order*. The front of the deque is always the index of the window maximum.\n\n\`\`\`python\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef window_max(values, k):\n    dq = deque()  # indices, values[dq[0]] >= values[dq[1]] >= ...\n    result = []\n    for i, v in enumerate(values):\n        # 1. Remove indices that have fallen out of the window\n        while dq and dq[0] < i - k + 1:\n            dq.popleft()\n        # 2. Remove indices whose values are <= v (they can never be a future max)\n        while dq and values[dq[-1]] <= v:\n            dq.pop()\n        # 3. Add the current index\n        dq.append(i)\n        # 4. The window max is always at the front\n        if i >= k - 1:\n            result.append(values[dq[0]])\n    return result\n\`\`\`\n\n**Trace on values=[1,3,1,2,5,3], k=3:**\n\n- i=0 (v=1): dq=[0]\n- i=1 (v=3): values[0]=1 <= 3, pop 0. dq=[1]\n- i=2 (v=1): values[1]=3 > 1, keep. dq=[1,2]. i>=k-1: window [1,3,1], max=values[1]=**3**\n- i=3 (v=2): values[2]=1 <= 2, pop 2. values[1]=3 > 2, keep. dq=[1,3]. Window [3,1,2], max=values[1]=**3**\n- i=4 (v=5): dq[0]=1 < 4-3+1=2, pop 1. values[3]=2 <= 5, pop 3. dq=[4]. Window [1,2,5], max=**5**\n- i=5 (v=3): values[4]=5 > 3, keep. dq=[4,5]. Window [2,5,3], max=values[4]=**5**\n\n**Result: [3, 3, 5, 5]**\n\n**Why O(n):** each index is pushed to the deque at most once and popped at most once. The inner while loops do not make the algorithm O(n²) — they amortize across all iterations.\n\n## What these patterns share\n\nBoth the heap and the monotonic deque maintain a structural invariant that makes the \"what is the best element?\" query O(1) or O(log n). The cost is paid during insertions, not queries. Recognizing which invariant to maintain is the interview skill — the code follows mechanically once you name it.\n\n## Common mistakes during interviews\n\n**Forgetting to evict expired indices from the deque front:** the deque can hold indices from earlier windows. Check `dq[0] < i - k + 1` at the start of each step.\n\n**Processing the max before the window is full:** `result.append` should only fire when `i >= k - 1`.\n\n**Using a sorted list instead of a deque:** sorted insertion is O(log n) per element but the deque approach is O(1) amortized — a meaningful difference at scale.",
