      contentMarkdown:
        "## What a trie is\n\nA **trie** (pronounced 'try', from re**trie**val), also called a **prefix tree**, is a tree where each edge is labeled with a character and each path from the root spells out a string. Instead of storing whole words in nodes, a trie stores them as *paths*: the word 'cat' is the path root → c → a → t. Words that share a prefix share the top of their paths, which is exactly what makes a trie fast for prefix questions.\n\nThis builds directly on the trees module (this module's prerequisite): a trie is a tree you traverse with DFS, but the branching is keyed by the next character rather than left/right children.\n\n## The structure\n\nEach node holds two things:\n\n1. **children** — a map from a character to the child node for that character. In interviews a Python `dict` (or a 26-slot array for lowercase-only alphabets) is standard.\n2. **is_word** — a boolean marking whether a complete inserted word *ends* at this node.\n\nThat second field is the subtle, essential part. Consider inserting 'car' and 'card':\n\n```\nroot\n └─ c ─ a ─ r(is_word=True) ─ d(is_word=True)\n```\n\nThe node after 'r' has `is_word=True` because 'car' was inserted; the node after 'd' also has `is_word=True` because 'card' was inserted. The node after 'a' has `is_word=False` — 'ca' is a prefix that leads somewhere, but it is not itself a stored word. **Without the is_word flag you cannot tell a stored word from an internal prefix** — that distinction is the whole point of the flag.\n\n## The three core operations\n\n```python\nclass TrieNode:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.children = {}      # char -> TrieNode\n        self.is_word = False\n\nclass Trie:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.root = TrieNode()\n\n    def insert(self, word):                 # O(len(word))\n        node = self.root\n        for ch in word:\n            if ch not in node.children:\n                node.children[ch] = TrieNode()\n            node = node.children[ch]\n        node.is_word = True                  # mark the end of a word\n\n    def search(self, word):                  # exact word? O(len(word))\n        node = self._walk(word)\n        return node is not None and node.is_word\n\n    def starts_with(self, prefix):           # any word with this prefix? O(len(prefix))\n        return self._walk(prefix) is not None\n\n    def _walk(self, s):                       # follow s; return end node or None\n        node = self.root\n        for ch in s:\n            if ch not in node.children:\n                return None\n            node = node.children[ch]\n        return node\n```\n\nNotice `search` and `starts_with` differ by exactly one thing: `search` additionally requires `is_word` on the final node. Both walk the string once — **O(length of the string), independent of how many words are stored.**\n\n## Why a trie instead of a hash set?\n\nA hash set answers 'is this exact string stored?' in O(length) too — so for *exact* membership a hash set is simpler and usually preferred. The trie earns its place when you need **prefix** operations:\n\n- 'How many stored words start with `app`?' — walk to the `app` node, done. A hash set would have to scan every key.\n- 'Give me all completions of `ap`.' — DFS the subtree under the `ap` node.\n- 'What is the shortest stored word that is a prefix of `cattle`?' — walk `cattle` and stop at the first is_word node.\n\nAll of these are local to a small part of the trie; a hash set makes you examine every key. The trie trades some memory (a node per distinct prefix character) for O(prefix-length) prefix queries.\n\n## Complexity\n\n- **Insert / exact search / prefix check:** O(L) where L is the length of the key — crucially *not* dependent on the number of stored words N.\n- **Space:** O(total characters across all inserted words) in the worst case, less when words share prefixes.\n- **Collect all words under a prefix:** O(size of that subtree).\n\n## Why interviewers ask about tries\n\nTries show up whenever prefixes matter: autocomplete, spell-check, IP routing tables, and word-search puzzles. The interview signal is whether you reach for a trie the moment 'prefix' appears, whether you correctly separate is_word from mere path existence, and whether you can DFS the subtree for completions.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\nOmitting the is_word flag and reporting every prefix as a stored word. Rebuilding strings character-by-character during a subtree DFS instead of storing the word (or accumulating the path) — wasteful. Reaching for a trie when a hash set suffices (exact membership with no prefix queries). Forgetting that a missing character mid-walk means the prefix/word is absent — return None/False immediately.\n\n## Next step\n\nThe walkthrough builds a trie from a small word list and traces insert, exact search, prefix check, and a subtree DFS for completions, node by node.",
