      estimatedMinutes: 20,
      contentMarkdown:
        "## Goal\n\nBuild a trie from the words `['cat', 'car', 'card', 'dog']`, then trace an exact search, a prefix check, and a subtree DFS that collects all completions of a prefix. Watching the nodes light up is the fastest way to internalize why prefix operations are cheap.\n\n## Step 1: insert the words\n\nInserting each word walks (or creates) one node per character and sets is_word on the last node.\n\n```\nAfter inserting 'cat', 'car', 'card', 'dog':\n\nroot\n ├─ c ── a ─┬─ t(is_word)              # cat\n │           └─ r(is_word) ── d(is_word) # car, card\n └─ d ── o ── g(is_word)                 # dog\n```\n\nKey observations:\n- 'cat', 'car', 'card' all share the path `c → a`, so that prefix is stored once.\n- The node after `a` on the c-branch has is_word=False — 'ca' is a real path but not a stored word.\n- The node after `r` has is_word=True ('car') AND has a child `d` leading to 'card'. A node can be both the end of one word and the middle of another.\n\n## Step 2: exact search — search('car')\n\nWalk from the root following c → a → r. Every character exists, and the final node (after `r`) has is_word=True → **found**.\n\nNow search('ca'): walk c → a. The path exists, but the node after `a` has is_word=False → **not found** (it is a prefix, not a stored word). This is the is_word flag doing its job.\n\nNow search('care'): walk c → a → r → e. After `r`, there is no child `e` → the walk falls off the trie → **not found**. A missing character means immediate failure.\n\n## Step 3: prefix check — starts_with('car')\n\nSame walk c → a → r, but we do **not** check is_word — we only need the path to exist. It does → **True**. Note starts_with('car') is True and search('ca') is False; the two operations differ only by the is_word test on the final node.\n\n## Step 4: collect completions — all words under prefix 'ca'\n\nThis is the operation a hash set cannot do cheaply. Walk to the `a` node on the c-branch (the prefix 'ca'), then DFS the subtree, recording a word every time you hit an is_word node:\n\n```\nStart at node 'ca'. DFS:\n  visit 'ca' (is_word=False) → skip\n    child 't': node 'cat' (is_word=True) → record 'cat'\n    child 'r': node 'car' (is_word=True) → record 'car'\n      child 'd': node 'card' (is_word=True) → record 'card'\nResult: ['cat', 'car', 'card']\n```\n\nThe DFS visits only the subtree under 'ca' — it never touches the 'dog' branch. That locality is exactly why a trie beats scanning all keys: the cost is proportional to the matching subtree, not the whole dictionary.\n\n## Step 5: accumulate the path or store the word?\n\nDuring the completion DFS you need the full word at each is_word node. Two options: (a) pass an accumulating prefix string down the recursion ('c', 'ca', 'cat', …) and record it when is_word is hit, or (b) store the complete word on the terminal node at insert time and read it directly. Option (b) trades a little memory for simpler, faster collection and is common in latency-sensitive code like the autocomplete problem.\n\n## The through-line\n\nEvery trie operation is 'walk the string, then either check a flag or DFS the subtree'. Exact search checks is_word; prefix check just needs the path; completion is a subtree DFS; the shortest-root problem stops at the first is_word during the walk. Once the picture above is in your head, each variant is a two-line change.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\nTreating a node that exists as a found word (skipping the is_word check). DFSing the whole trie instead of starting at the prefix node. Rebuilding strings by concatenation at every node when the word could be stored once at the terminal. Forgetting that a node can be both a word end and an internal node (the 'car'/'card' case).",
