      estimatedMinutes: 22,
      contentMarkdown:
        "## Goal\n\nTrace the choose/explore/un-choose template on subsets and permutations decision tree in hand, then see how a single pruning guard turns combination sum and N-queens from 'generate everything' into a tight search.\n\n## Part 1: subsets of [1, 2, 3] via the start-index pattern\n\n```python\ndef subsets(nums):\n    res = []\n    def dfs(start, path):\n        res.append(path[:])            # record a COPY at every node\n        for i in range(start, len(nums)):\n            path.append(nums[i])       # CHOOSE\n            dfs(i + 1, path)           # EXPLORE (i+1: never reuse earlier indices)\n            path.pop()                 # UN-CHOOSE\n    dfs(0, [])\n    return res\n```\n\nThe decision tree, with the recorded subset at each node:\n\n```\ndfs(0, [])                     record []\n├─ choose 1 → dfs(1, [1])      record [1]\n│  ├─ choose 2 → dfs(2,[1,2])  record [1,2]\n│  │  └─ choose 3 → [1,2,3]    record [1,2,3]\n│  └─ choose 3 → dfs(3,[1,3])  record [1,3]\n├─ choose 2 → dfs(2, [2])      record [2]\n│  └─ choose 3 → [2,3]         record [2,3]\n└─ choose 3 → dfs(3, [3])      record [3]\n```\n\nEight subsets, one per node. The **start index** (`i + 1`) is what stops [1,2] and [2,1] from both appearing — we only ever extend with elements to the *right* of the last chosen index, so each subset is generated exactly once. The `path.pop()` after each recursive call is the un-choose that keeps `path` correct for the next sibling.\n\n## Part 2: permutations of [1, 2, 3] with a used-set\n\nSubsets pick a growing suffix; permutations must use *every* element, in every order, so the constraint is different — track which elements are already used:\n\n```python\ndef permutations(nums):\n    res = []\n    used = [False] * len(nums)\n    def dfs(path):\n        if len(path) == len(nums):\n            res.append(path[:]); return    # a complete ordering\n        for i in range(len(nums)):\n            if used[i]:\n                continue                    # prune: element already placed\n            used[i] = True; path.append(nums[i])    # CHOOSE\n            dfs(path)                                # EXPLORE\n            path.pop(); used[i] = False              # UN-CHOOSE (both!)\n    dfs([])\n    return res\n```\n\nThe tree branches into every unused element at each depth:\n\n```\n[]\n├─ 1 → [1]      ├─ 2 → [1,2] → [1,2,3]\n│               └─ 3 → [1,3] → [1,3,2]\n├─ 2 → [2]      ├─ 1 → [2,1] → [2,1,3]\n│               └─ 3 → [2,3] → [2,3,1]\n└─ 3 → [3]      ├─ 1 → [3,1] → [3,1,2]\n                └─ 2 → [3,2] → [3,2,1]\n```\n\n3! = 6 leaves. Note the un-choose here resets **two** pieces of state: pop the path AND clear used[i]. Miss either and later branches are corrupted.\n\n## Part 3: pruning makes combination sum tractable\n\nCombination sum (numbers reusable, all combos summing to target) uses the same start-index skeleton as subsets, plus two changes: recurse with `i` (not `i+1`) to allow reuse, and **prune** on a sorted array.\n\n```python\ndef combination_sum(candidates, target):\n    candidates.sort()\n    res = []\n    def dfs(start, remaining, path):\n        if remaining == 0:\n            res.append(path[:]); return\n        for i in range(start, len(candidates)):\n            if candidates[i] > remaining:\n                break                       # PRUNE: sorted, so all later are too big\n            path.append(candidates[i])\n            dfs(i, remaining - candidates[i], path)   # i, not i+1 → reuse allowed\n            path.pop()\n    dfs(0, target, [])\n    return res\n```\n\nThe `break` is the whole game: on `[2,3,6,7]` with target 7, once the current candidate exceeds the remaining target, every later candidate (larger, since sorted) is hopeless — we cut the entire rest of the loop instead of recursing into dead branches.\n\n## Part 4: pruning in two dimensions — N-queens\n\nN-queens places one queen per row (which removes the same-row constraint for free) and prunes columns and diagonals as it descends:\n\n```python\ndef count_n_queens(n):\n    cols, diag, anti = set(), set(), set()\n    count = 0\n    def place(row):\n        nonlocal count\n        if row == n:\n            count += 1; return\n        for col in range(n):\n            if col in cols or (row-col) in diag or (row+col) in anti:\n                continue                    # PRUNE: attacked square\n            cols.add(col); diag.add(row-col); anti.add(row+col)   # CHOOSE\n            place(row + 1)                                        # EXPLORE\n            cols.discard(col); diag.discard(row-col); anti.discard(row+col)  # UN-CHOOSE\n    place(0)\n    return count\n```\n\nThe insight most candidates miss: a diagonal is identified by a *constant* `row - col`, and an anti-diagonal by a constant `row + col`. Tracking those two values in sets makes each 'is this square attacked?' check O(1), and the three `continue` guards prune every attacked placement before recursing.\n\n## The through-line\n\nEvery one of these is the same loop: choose a value, recurse, un-choose. Subsets constrain with a start index; permutations with a used-set; combination sum adds a sorted-array break; N-queens adds column/diagonal sets. Learn the skeleton once, and each problem is a different `valid()` guard and a different 'what does a complete solution look like?' check.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\nUn-choosing only part of the state (e.g., popping the path but forgetting used[i] in permutations). Using i+1 in combination sum and losing reuse, or index 0 and generating duplicate orderings. Skipping the sorted-array break and timing out. In N-queens, scanning all placed queens for diagonal conflicts (O(n)) instead of the O(1) (row±col) sets.",
