      estimatedMinutes: 22,
      contentMarkdown:
        "## What a greedy algorithm is\n\nA **greedy algorithm** builds a solution one step at a time, always taking the choice that looks best *right now* — the locally optimal move — and never reconsidering it. No backtracking, no exploring alternatives. When it works, it is usually the simplest and fastest approach: often O(n) or O(n log n) with a sort.\n\nThe catch: greedy does not always work. The entire skill is (1) recognizing when the locally optimal choice is also globally optimal, and (2) being able to *argue* why. An interviewer will not accept 'it seemed to work on the examples' — they want the justification.\n\n## Greedy vs dynamic programming\n\nBoth greedy and DP (the next module) solve optimization problems with optimal substructure. The difference is decisive:\n\n- **DP** considers *all* choices at each step and keeps the best, because a locally suboptimal choice can lead to a globally better outcome. It pays for this with more time and memory.\n- **Greedy** commits to *one* choice at each step and never revisits it. It only works when that single choice is provably safe.\n\nIf you cannot argue the greedy choice is safe, reach for DP. Coin change with arbitrary denominations is the classic cautionary tale: greedily taking the largest coin fails for coins [1, 3, 4] making 6 (greedy: 4+1+1 = 3 coins; optimal: 3+3 = 2 coins). The same problem with canonical currency denominations *is* greedy-safe — the denominations are the difference.\n\n## The two proof techniques\n\n**1. The exchange argument (the workhorse).** Assume some optimal solution differs from the greedy one. Show you can *exchange* a piece of the optimal solution for the greedy choice without making it worse. Repeating the exchange transforms the optimal solution into the greedy one while preserving optimality — so the greedy solution is also optimal.\n\nExample — activity selection (keep the most non-overlapping intervals): greedy keeps the interval that *finishes earliest*. Exchange argument: take any optimal set; its first-ending interval finishes no earlier than the greedy's earliest-finisher, so swapping in the greedy choice frees at least as much room for the rest — the swapped solution is still valid and just as large. Hence greedy is optimal.\n\n**2. 'Greedy stays ahead'.** Show that after each step, the greedy solution is at least as good as any other solution's corresponding step — greedy is never behind, so it cannot end up worse.\n\n## The usual shape of a greedy solution\n\nMost greedy solutions have one of these forms:\n\n1. **Sort, then sweep.** Sort by the key that makes the greedy choice obvious (earliest end time, smallest cost, largest ratio), then make one linear pass. Assign-cookies, activity selection, and erase-overlaps all follow this.\n2. **Sweep while tracking a running quantity.** No sort needed; maintain a running sum/reach/tank and make a decision based on it. Jump game (farthest reach), gas station (running tank), stock profit (sum of up-moves) follow this.\n\nThe hard part is almost always identifying the *right* sort key or the *right* running quantity — that choice encodes the exchange argument.\n\n## A recognition checklist\n\nGreedy is plausible when: the problem asks for a single optimum (min/max count, min cost); a natural ordering exists (by end time, by ratio, by size); and committing to the best-looking local choice does not obviously destroy future options. Always sanity-check with a small potential counterexample before committing — if you find one, switch to DP.\n\n## Why interviewers ask it\n\nGreedy problems test judgment and proof, not just coding. Two candidates can write the same three lines; the one who states the exchange argument and names a case where greedy would fail demonstrates real understanding. Interviewers frequently ask 'why is this correct?' and 'when would greedy fail here?' as the actual evaluation.\n\n## Common mistakes\n\nAssuming greedy works because it passes the given examples, without an argument. Choosing the wrong greedy key (e.g., sorting activities by start or by duration instead of by end). Applying greedy to a problem that needs DP (arbitrary-denomination coin change, 0/1 knapsack). Not identifying the running quantity, and reaching for O(n²) when an O(n) sweep exists.\n\n## Next step\n\nThe walkthrough works the two dominant patterns end to end: an interval greedy proved by exchange (activity selection / erase-overlaps) and a running-quantity sweep (jump game and gas station).",
